{"id": "tea:eknathi-bhagavata:2.uttama-bhagavata", "source": "src:eknathi-bhagavata", "location": {"ref": "ch.2 (on BhP 11.2.45)"}, "paraphrase": "The best devotee sees the Lord in all beings and all beings in the Lord; Eknāth makes this vision the mark of the true bhāgavata.", "tags": {"level": "bridging", "standpoint": "devotional", "naya": null, "path": ["devotion"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["ethics", "ultimate"], "teachers": ["tch:eknath"], "cross_refs": ["tea:bhagavata-purana:11.2.45"], "concepts": ["cpt:marks-of-the-saint"], "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U26-regional-bhakti"], "disagreements": []}, "coverage": ["A9"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U26-regional-bhakti"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:eknathi-bhagavata:5.kali-nama", "source": "src:eknathi-bhagavata", "location": {"ref": "ch.5 (on BhP 11.5.32–36)"}, "paraphrase": "In the Kali age the wise worship the Lord by singing his name together; Eknāth teaches that the name alone suffices in Kali, without sacrifice or austerity.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["sound", "devotion"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice", "world-fate"], "teachers": ["tch:eknath"], "concepts": ["cpt:kali-yuga-dharma", "cpt:divine-name"], "practices": ["prc:nama-sankirtana"], "cross_refs": ["tea:bhagavata-purana:11.5.32"], "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U26-regional-bhakti"], "disagreements": []}, "coverage": ["A9"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U26-regional-bhakti"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:eknathi-bhagavata:7-9.avadhuta", "source": "src:eknathi-bhagavata", "location": {"ref": "chs. 7–9 (on BhP 11.7–9)"}, "paraphrase": "The avadhūta tells King Yadu of his twenty-four teachers — earth, air, sky, water, fire, moon, sun, pigeon, python, ocean, moth, bee, elephant, honey-gatherer, deer, fish, the courtesan Piṅgalā, osprey, child, maiden, arrow-maker, serpent, spider and wasp — each teaching detachment; Eknāth draws from each a lesson for the devotee.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "intermediate"}, "types": ["ethics", "practice"], "teachers": ["tch:eknath"], "cross_refs": ["tea:bhagavata-purana:11.7-9"], "concepts": ["cpt:vairagya"], "reported_by_opponent": false, "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U26-regional-bhakti"], "disagreements": []}, "coverage": ["A9"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U26-regional-bhakti"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
